

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Noted — one shared module, used by both.

  Next fork: [1mwhere the logs actually go.[0m This is the decision that determines whether "debug production issues" is
  satisfied, and it's the expensive one to change later, because it sets the logger's interface (sync call vs.
  buffered/async with flush) and whether there's a server-side piece to build.

  - [1mConsole only, structured[0m — replace ad-hoc [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m with levelled, JSON-shaped records. Cheap, no dependency, no
    backend. Node side is genuinely production-usable this way (stdout gets collected by whatever runs it). Browser side
    only helps when you can ask a user to open devtools — it does [3mnot[0m let you debug sessions you can't reproduce.
  - [1mConsole + remote sink for the browser[0m — same records, plus the browser batches them and POSTs to a collector
    endpoint. This is what actually answers "debug production issues" for the login flow, but it needs an endpoint that
    exists (you have none today — [38;5;153mapi.example.com[39m is a stub), plus batching, failure handling, and a hard rule about
    never shipping passwords.
  - [1mConsole now, pluggable transport seam[0m — structured records and one documented hook where a sink gets attached later.
    Middle cost, keeps the remote option open without committing to an endpoint that doesn't exist yet.

  My recommendation is the third. The remote sink is the only thing that truly delivers production debuggability, but
  it's blocked on a collector you don't have, and guessing its contract now is the part most likely to be thrown away.
  If you already have somewhere to send logs, tell me and I'll take option two instead.
